namespace pdg {
/**
\class PhysicsBody
\ingroup Physics
\brief Optional physical motion for a Sprite or Part, using seconds.

Create through sprite.setupPhysicsBody(mass, momentOfInertia) or the matching
Part method. Their physics property returns the body, or shared
pdg.PhysicsBody.NoPhysics when absent. Queries do not allocate. NoPhysics safely ignores
physical commands, with a diagnostic in debug builds. The physics property is
read-only: callers can operate on the returned body but cannot replace it by
assignment. Use setupPhysicsBody()/removePhysicsBody() to change the association.
Use identity comparison with pdg.PhysicsBody.NoPhysics to test absence. The pdg.NoPhysics
export refers to the same singleton. getSolver() returns a physicsSolver_ constant.
Layers and Animated have no physics property or physical methods.

\code{.js}
const body = sprite.setupPhysicsBody(2, 4);
body.applyImpulse(new pdg.Vector(4, 0)); // immediate momentum change
body.applyForce(new pdg.Vector(8, 0), 0.25); // force for a quarter second
body.applyAngularImpulse(2);
body.applyTorque(8, 0.25);
\endcode

Linear impulse changes velocity by impulse/mass; angular impulse changes angular
velocity by impulse/momentOfInertia. Force and torque integrate over a finite
duration, in floating-point seconds. Zero duration contributes nothing. Continuous
loads have explicit add/remove methods. Physical linear/angular damping is an
exponential rate per second; contact friction and restitution are separate.

State and vectors use owning-layer/world coordinates, radians and seconds.
An optional application Point contributes angular response around the center of
mass. Off-center force captures the lever arm at submission. In JavaScript the
optional worldPoint follows durationSeconds and delaySeconds in applyForce().

Sprite bodies use Chipmunk when its layer enables it, otherwise PDG's basic
integrator. Unbound Part bodies also join the Chipmunk world on a physics-enabled
layer; otherwise they use the basic solver. Optional Collider geometry supplies
contacts for Sprite and Part bodies alike. The basic solver adds approximate
linear/angular contact impulses, Coulomb friction, penetration correction and
body-to-body constraints. Chipmunk uses its native shapes and constraints.
Free motion and timed loads remain analytically integrated by the basic solver.
Numerical trajectories can differ with timestep and solver settings.

Owners advance their bodies automatically. removePhysicsBody() returns the
owner to NoPhysics and detaches retained body references. A detached basic body
can be advanced explicitly with step(seconds). Dynamic bodies reject programmed
movement/spin on their owner; immediate transform setters teleport. Growth and
stretching remain Animated operations. Set inertia explicitly when appropriate.

\see Sprite
\see Part
\see Animated
*/

/**
\var PhysicsBody::NoPhysics
\brief Shared absence object returned by Sprite.physics and Part.physics until a body is created.

Compare by identity to test for absence. Use removePhysicsBody() to restore
the shared absence object. Valid mutating calls are ignored; they do not change the singleton or queue
work for later body creation.

\see Sprite
\see Part
\see Animated
*/
}
